{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Extract a compact state card from the selected raw sessions.\nUse only the selected sessions and the query. Do not infer hidden metadata or\nadd facts outside the evidence.\n\nSelected evidence sessions:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I’ve been thinking a lot about what makes a home feel calm and functional when you’re not living alone—any tips on setting good household routines and boundaries without making things feel rigid?\n[assistant] It helps to think of routines and boundaries less like rules and more like gentle rhythms everyone can settle into.\n\nA good start is talking openly about what each person needs to feel comfortable at home—quiet hours, shared spaces, chores, alone time—without trying to lock anything in stone. Framing it as “what makes things easier for all of us?” keeps it collaborative instead of rigid.\n\nLight, flexible routines work well. For example, agreeing on a general window for cleaning shared areas, or a simple habit like putting dishes straight into the dishwasher instead of the sink. These aren’t strict policies, just habits that reduce friction.\n\nBoundaries feel softer when you explain the “why.” Saying something like “I need a bit of quiet after work to decompress” invites understanding, and it makes it easier for others to share their needs too. The more everyone feels heard, the less anyone feels policed.\n\nAnd revisiting things occasionally helps. Homes change, schedules shift, and checking in every now and then keeps the whole setup feeling cooperative instead of fixed.\n\nIt’s really about creating a space where everyone feels considered, not controlled.\n[user] That makes sense. We’re four people sharing the space, so the friction points are usually dishes/kitchen mess, noise when someone’s trying to rest, and privacy (like people walking into rooms without knocking). How would you suggest we set up a simple “house agreement” that covers those without turning into a big argument?\n[assistant] You can frame a house agreement as a shared goal rather than a set of rules. Something like, “Let’s make a quick plan so the place stays comfortable for all of us,” keeps the tone light and cooperative.\n\nFor the kitchen, it helps to agree on the simplest possible habit everyone can realistically follow—maybe rinsing and clearing dishes right after use, or agreeing that the counters get wiped before the end of the evening. Keep it small enough that no one feels micromanaged.\n\nFor noise, you can all choose a general quiet window, not a strict curfew. Just saying, “If someone’s resting, let’s keep the volume down and use headphones,” communicates the expectation without policing anyone.\n\nFor privacy, make knocking a house norm by treating it like basic courtesy rather than a rule. A casual line like, “Let’s all knock before entering closed doors so no one gets startled,” is enough.\n\nThe key is to keep the conversation short, calm, and future‑focused. Instead of rehashing past annoyances, talk about what will make things smoother going forward. If you keep the agreement simple and based on shared comfort, it usually avoids turning into an argument.\n[user] Could you help me draft a one-page “house agreement” we can post on the fridge—like 6–8 bullet points covering kitchen/dishes, quiet hours, knocking/privacy, and a simple way to handle slip-ups without blaming anyone?\n[assistant] Here’s a simple, friendly house agreement you can put on the fridge:\n\n• Keep the kitchen tidy by rinsing dishes after use and leaving counters clear when you’re done cooking.  \n• Try to avoid leaving shared‑space messes overnight unless you’ve checked in with others.  \n• Keep noise low during agreed quiet hours, and use headphones when someone is resting.  \n• Let housemates know if you’re having guests over, especially in shared spaces.  \n• Knock before entering any closed door and wait for a reply.  \n• Respect each other’s need for alone time—closed doors mean someone is recharging.  \n• If something slips through the cracks, bring it up gently and focus on fixing it, not blaming.  \n• Check in every couple of weeks to adjust anything that isn’t working and keep things smooth for everyone.\n[user] My parents and my brother live with me, so it's a pretty full house with four of us. Could you tweak that fridge “house agreement” so it feels respectful across a parent–adult kid dynamic (not like I’m telling them what to do), while still being clear about the kitchen/dishes, quiet hours, and knocking/privacy?\n[assistant] Here’s a version that feels more like a shared family understanding than a set of instructions, while still being clear and respectful:\n\n• Let’s all try to leave the kitchen in a “reset” state—dishes rinsed, counters wiped—so it’s easy for the next person to use.  \n• If someone’s in the middle of cooking or tired, it’s fine to leave things for a bit; just give a heads‑up so no one is surprised.  \n• We’ll keep things quieter during the usual rest times, and if someone is napping or winding down, we’ll try to use softer voices or headphones.  \n• Closed doors mean someone needs a little privacy, so we’ll knock and wait before entering.  \n• If something feels off—messes, noise, or anything else—we’ll bring it up kindly and focus on solving it rather than pointing fingers.  \n• Every now and then, we can do a quick check‑in to see if anything needs adjusting so the house feels comfortable for all of us.\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I’ve been thinking a lot about what it actually takes to pick up your life and move to a new city for a long-ish stint—logistics, emotions, the whole reset. If you were advising someone planning a year abroad, what would you prioritize first: housing, budgeting, social life, or work setup?\n[assistant] I’d start with housing, simply because it affects everything else. Knowing where you’ll live, what it costs, and what your day‑to‑day environment will feel like gives you a stable foundation for the rest of the move.\n\nOnce you have that pinned down, budgeting comes into focus naturally, since you’ll have a clearer sense of fixed costs. Work setup tends to fall into place after that, especially if you already know the nature of your job or program. Social life is something I’d treat as an ongoing process rather than a pre‑move task—you can’t fully build it until you’re there, but you can set yourself up by choosing a neighborhood or housing style that makes meeting people easier.\n\nSo: get the roof and the rhythm first, and the rest becomes much simpler to shape.\n[user] That makes sense. I finally signed the papers on that year-long fellowship in Copenhagen, so I’ve been selling off most of my furniture and setting up a tiny sublet near the lab. Given that, what would you tackle next in the first month on the ground to make the transition smoother—especially around budgeting and building a social routine without letting the work setup consume everything?\n[assistant] Once you’ve landed and settled into the sublet, I’d spend the first month getting a feel for your actual cost of living. Track what you spend without trying to optimize too hard at first; after a couple of weeks you’ll see the real patterns and can adjust your budget in a way that feels natural instead of forced.\n\nAt the same time, start weaving in small, repeatable social habits. Pick one or two things you can do weekly—grabbing coffee at the same spot, joining a casual meetup, showing up to a lab social hour—and let those become anchors. They don’t have to be big gestures; consistency is what creates familiarity and makes a new city feel less like you’re floating.\n\nFor work, set clear boundaries right away. Decide what your “default” workday looks like, both start and stop, and hold yourself to it unless there’s a genuine exception. It’s much easier to loosen boundaries later than to tighten them once you’ve slipped into overwork.\n\nIf you manage those three—honest budgeting, small social routines, and early work boundaries—you’ll settle in far more smoothly than you expect.\n[user] Could you help me sketch a simple “first 30 days” plan for Copenhagen—like a weekly checklist that covers: (1) getting my budget calibrated (banking, recurring costs, groceries/transport), (2) building a social routine that doesn’t feel forced, and (3) setting work boundaries so I don’t just live at the lab?\n[assistant] Here’s a simple way to frame the first month without leaning too hard on list formatting.\n\nFor week one, sort out the basics: get your banking fully set up, note your fixed costs, figure out your commute, and do a few grocery runs to understand local prices. Treat it as data‑gathering rather than optimization.\n\nIn week two, start shaping a rough budget based on what you actually spent in week one. At the same time, pick one low‑effort social habit, like returning to the same café or attending one recurring event near your neighborhood. Keep it light so it doesn’t feel like a project.\n\nWeek three is when you tighten things a bit. Adjust your budget so it matches reality, not aspiration. Add one more social touchpoint—maybe a lab social hour or a casual meetup—just enough to create a rhythm. This is also when you set a default workday: a start and stop time you stick to unless there’s a real reason not to.\n\nBy week four, you’re mostly refining. See if your spending feels sustainable, tweak anything that’s off, and notice which social habits feel natural. Reinforce the work boundaries you set, because this is when they’re most likely to slip as you get more comfortable in the lab.\n\nIf you hit those marks each week, you’ll feel settled without overloading yourself.\n[user] Can you turn that into a clearer week-by-week checklist (like 5–8 bullets per week) and add a couple Copenhagen-specific ideas for low-pressure social routines—especially if I’m not a big nightlife person?\n[assistant] Week 1  \n• Open or finalize local banking and make sure your payments/transfer options work.  \n• List all fixed costs: rent, utilities, transit pass, phone.  \n• Do two or three grocery runs to map out prices.  \n• Try different transport routes to the lab to see what feels best.  \n• Walk your neighborhood so you know cafés, parks, and basic shops.\n\nWeek 2  \n• Review week‑one spending and sketch a rough budget.  \n• Get a Rejsekort or monthly transit card if you haven’t yet.  \n• Establish one low‑pressure social habit, like a weekly visit to the same café.  \n• Attend one lab social or informal lunch.  \n• Define your default work hours and stick to them for the week.\n\nWeek 3  \n• Adjust your budget based on actual patterns.  \n• Identify one or two “go‑to” grocery stores for price and convenience.  \n• Add one more social touchpoint, like a casual meetup or interest group.  \n• Protect your work stop‑time and avoid “just one more hour.”  \n• Try a new walking route or bike path to explore more of the city.\n\nWeek 4  \n• Re‑check spending and fine‑tune anything that feels off.  \n• Start building a simple meal routine that fits your budget.  \n• Keep the social habits that feel easiest and drop the rest.  \n• Recommit to work boundaries before habits calcify.  \n• Plan one small weekend outing to a new neighborhood.\n\nCopenhagen‑specific low‑pressure social ideas  \n• Join a weekly “language café” at a library—they’re relaxed and mostly about chatting.  \n• Visit outdoor swimming spots or saunas like Islands Brygge or Nordhavn; people often chat casually.  \n• Drop into community events at Kulturhuset Indre By or local library talks.  \n• Join a casual cycling group or beginner‑friendly running club; very low nightlife energy.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still live day-to-day in the same home as their parents and brother?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these three fields:\n{\n  \"old_state\": \"the earlier relevant state supported by the sessions\",\n  \"new_state\": \"the later relevant state supported by the sessions\",\n  \"change_type\": \"CO_REFERENTIAL | PROPAGATED | UNKNOWN\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n- old_state and new_state must be non-empty strings.\n- Use CO_REFERENTIAL when the same attribute changes directly.\n- Use PROPAGATED when one observed change indirectly invalidates a related state.\n- Use UNKNOWN when the relationship cannot be established from the sessions.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}